d the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n57:    - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n58:    - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n59:    - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n60:    - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n61:    - The run verdict has three readers: this step, the @code RETURN `Quality Gate` field and `sk-code-review`, which reads it as input evidence only.\n62: 3. Name the claim to prove and the command, action or observation that proves it.\n63: 4. Select the surface-appropriate command set and targeted tests from the active-surface guidance.\n64: 5. Run fresh commands and capture the command names, exit codes, test counts, important output and failures.\n65: 6. Add end-to-end, runtime or performance evidence when the claim depends on behavior outside static checks.\n66: 7. Compare the current evidence to the baseline and report the delta.\n67: 8. Run a mutation check or claim falsifier when the claim depends on a new or changed test.\n68: 9. Walk the Pre-Claim Gate below, then state only the claims the evidence proves. If the evidence fails, hand back without editing.\n69: \n70: ### Baseline And Delta\n71: \n72: Verification compares against the baseline whenever it states a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n73: \n74: | Field | Required Content |\n75: | --- | --- |\n76: | Baseline | Starting command status, failing tests, warning count, runtime issue, performance number or `UNKNOWN` when no baseline was captured. |\n77: | Current | Fresh output from the whole gate, not only the subset you iterated on: exit codes, test counts, runtime observations or measured numbers. |\n78: | Delta | What changed, improved, regressed or remains unproven, including the parts that did not move. |\n79: | Claim Scope | The exact claim the evidence supports and the blind spot that remains. |\n80: \n81: No baseline means no broad no-regressions claim. A narrower claim tied to fresh evidence still stands.\n82: \n83: The baseline floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 5 (BASELINES). This table is the sk-code record shape for it. Implementation records its Baseline row before the first edit.\n84: \n85: ### Verification Ladder\n86: \n87: Climb from the cheapest rung to the most authoritative and name what each rung cannot see:\n88: \n89: | Rung | Proves | Blind Spot |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | Unit | Isolated logic or assertion path | Integration, wiring, real I/O, runtime state |\n92: | In-memory | Multiple modules without external runtime | Real file system, serialization, deployed config, external state |\n93: | On-server | Runtime service or local server path | Deployment differences, caches, real user environment |\n94: | Live | Exact exercised path in real target | Unexercised paths, other users, other environments, future data |\n95: \n96: ### Mutation Check And Claim Falsifier\n97: \n98: After green evidence for a new or modified test, confirm the test can fail for the right reason when that is feasible. A true red result is an assertion failing against the intended behavior. A compile error, or a command that never reached the assertion, is not a satisfying red. If a test stays green after the guarded behavior is broken, report it as a verification defect and hand back.\n99: \n100: This applies the idea behind `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 4 (THE NEGATIVE CONTROL): a check that passes whether or not the behavior works proves nothing. The before-fix half of that control, reproducing the failure with the same check, is step 2 of the [Debug Loop](./workflow-debug.md#debug-loop).\n101: \n102: ### Pre-Claim Gate Substance\n103: \n104: Walk every gate in order before any status claim:\n105: \n106: | Gate | Required Discipline |\n107: | --- | --- |\n108: | Identify | Name the command, action or observation that proves the claim. |\n109: | Run | Execute the surface-appropriate verification commands in the current session. |\n110: | Test | Exercise targeted unit, integration, end-to-end, runtime or manual checks where available, including at least one edge case and one error path. |\n111: | Verify | Confirm the output, exit code, test count and observed behavior match expectations, and that the run reached the changed code on a fresh build with real assertions. |\n112: | Record | Save the exact evidence the final claim rests on. |\n113: | Reread | Reread the request and its amendments, and confirm each outcome it asks for is backed by recorded evidence or named as not done. |\n114: | Claim | State only the narrow claim the evidence supports. |\n115: \n116: The Verify row guards against the four ways a green run lies, which `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 3 lists: it did not run, it ran on the wrong thing, it ran on stale artifacts or it asserted nothing.\n117: \n118: ### OpenCode Verification Reality\n119: \n120: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [verification reality](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#3-verification-reality), including the `validate.sh` pointer, and the [runtime build traps](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#4-runtime-build-traps).\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n125: \n126: ### Always\n127: \n128: - Always run fresh verification in the current session before a completion-related claim.\n129: - Always resolve surface identity before selecting commands.\n130: - Always record command names, exit codes, key output, test counts and runtime observations.\n131: - Always report the baseline, current result, delta and claim scope for a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n132: - Always name the verification ladder rung reached and the blind spot that remains.\n133: - Always include behavior evidence when runtime behavior changed and tooling is available.\n134: - Always hand back when verification finds a defect.\n135: - Always confirm the diff scope matches the stated change, with no accidental extras or debug residue.\n136: - Always compare warnings against the last working state, so a new warning is reported and not absorbed.\n137: \n138: ### Never\n139: \n140: - Never edit or create files during verification.\n141: - Never dispatch subagents from verification.\n142: - Never claim done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready from unrun commands.\n143: - Never claim no regressions without a baseline and a delta.\n144: - Never treat one green rung as proof of a higher rung.\n145: - Never hide a failed or skipped verifier behind a positive summary.\n146: - Never invent runtime, test or performance evidence.\n147: - Never verify against stale build output. Clean and rebuild first when an artifact is suspect.\n148: - Never present a check run in one environment as proof for every environment.\n149: \n150: ### Escalate If\n151: \n152: - The surface or the required verification command set is unknown.\n153: - A required verifier path is missing or unsafe to run.\n154: - Commands contradict each other on a blocking outcome.\n155: - Runtime evidence is required but unavailable and the claim would mislead without it.\n156: - The baseline is missing and the requested claim needs a regression comparison.\n157: - A mutation check shows the test is vacuous.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n162: \n163: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives reproducible verification failures, failed commands, broken runtime checks or vacuous tests.\n164: - [Implementation](./workflow-implement.md) receives the handback when required behavior is missing or build work is incomplete.\n165: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the handback when author-side checks, comment hygiene or checklist issues block verification.\n166: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns critique when the user wants findings instead of final evidence.\n167: \n168: Verification is ready to report only when it ran last, used only non-mutating actions, recorded fresh active-surface evidence, compared against the baseline when the claim needed it, named its blind spots and handed back any defect without editing.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n173: \n174: - [workflow-implement.md](./workflow-implement.md) - the build phase whose baseline and handoff this phase reads.\n175: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that receives every failed verification.\n176: - [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - surface detection, which decides the command set.\n177: - [workflow-guardrails.md](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md) - the verification command chain for system code under `.skilled/`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-verify.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560446126,"end":1794560447252}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560447255,"sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4234d10001iquO3l375in3W3","sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","messageID":"msg_1d423427c001sNSrTmXA3lQTJf","type":"tool","id":"call_d610167de56c408986d71ce1","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/workflow-implement.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560445712,"end":1794560447255}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560447258,"sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4235192001JXC1bCf0AyI51G","sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","messageID":"msg_1d423427c001sNSrTmXA3lQTJf","type":"tool","id":"call_e1ccb6bb2c1b42a783128bea","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md, lines 1-409\n1: ---\n2: title: TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode typescript checklist\"\n6:   - \"typescript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"no any public api\"\n8:   - \"pascalcase types check\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a TypeScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for TypeScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to TypeScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning       | Enforcement                        |\n33: |-------|---------------|------------------------------------|\n34: | P0    | HARD BLOCKER  | Must fix before commit             |\n35: | P1    | Required      | Must fix or get explicit approval  |\n36: | P2    | Recommended   | Can defer with justification       |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### File Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has TypeScript module header identifying the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: A `.d.ts`, `.d.mts` or `.d.cts` file the compiler emitted, one that ends with a `//# sourceMappingURL=` line, is exempt. A hand-written declaration file still needs the header.\n51: \n52: **Required format**:\n53: ```typescript\n54: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n55: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n56: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n57: ```\n58: \n59: ### No `any` in Public API\n60: \n61: ```markdown\n62: [ ] No `any` type used in exported functions, interfaces, or type aliases\n63: ```\n64: \n65: **Correct**:\n66: ```typescript\n67: export function parse(input: unknown): SearchConfig { }\n68: export interface SearchOptions { metadata: Record<string, unknown>; }\n69: ```\n70: \n71: **Wrong**:\n72: ```typescript\n73: export function parse(input: any): any { }\n74: export interface SearchOptions { metadata: any; }\n75: ```\n76: \n77: **Exception**: Interop with untyped third-party modules requires justification comment:\n78: ```typescript\n79: // Interop: sqlite-vec has no type definitions\n80: // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\n81: const sqliteVec = require('sqlite-vec') as any;\n82: ```\n83: \n84: ### No Commented-Out Code\n85: \n86: ```markdown\n87: [ ] No commented-out code blocks present\n88: ```\n89: \n90: Same rule as JavaScript. Delete unused code; git preserves history.\n91: \n92: ### WHY Comments\n93: \n94: ```markdown\n95: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n96:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n97:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n98: ```\n99: \n100: Explain reasoning, not mechanics.\n101: \n102: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n103: \n104: ```markdown\n105: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n106: ```\n107: \n108: Same rule as JavaScript.\n109: \n110: ---\n111: \n112: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n113: \n114: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n115: \n116: ### PascalCase Types and Interfaces\n117: \n118: ```markdown\n119: [ ] All interfaces, type aliases, and enums use PascalCase naming\n120: ```\n121: \n122: **Correct**:\n123: ```typescript\n124: interface SearchResult { }\n125: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n126: enum ErrorCode { NotFound, Timeout }\n127: ```\n128: \n129: **Wrong**:\n130: ```typescript\n131: interface searchResult { }       // camelCase\n132: type memoryState = string;       // camelCase\n133: enum errorCode { }               // camelCase\n134: ```\n135: \n136: ### Explicit Return Types on Public Functions\n137: \n138: ```markdown\n139: [ ] All exported functions have explicit return type annotations\n140: ```\n141: \n142: **Correct**:\n143: ```typescript\n144: export function calculate(input: number): number { }\n145: export async function search(query: string): Promise<SearchResult[]> { }\n146: ```\n147: \n148: **Wrong**:\n149: ```typescript\n150: export function calculate(input: number) { }      // inferred return\n151: export async function search(query: string) { }    // inferred return\n152: ```\n153: \n154: Private/internal helper functions may use inferred return types.\n155: \n156: ### Interfaces for Data Shapes\n157: \n158: ```markdown\n159: [ ] Object shapes passed across module boundaries use named interfaces\n160: ```\n161: \n162: **Correct**:\n163: ```typescript\n164: interface SearchOptions {\n165:   limit: number;\n166:   offset: number;\n167: }\n168: function search(query: string, options: SearchOptions): void { }\n169: ```\n170: \n171: **Wrong**:\n172: ```typescript\n173: function search(query: string, options: { limit: number; offset: number }): void { }\n174: ```\n175: \n176: ### Strict Mode Enabled\n177: \n178: ```markdown\n179: [ ] tsconfig.json has \"strict\": true\n180: ```\n181: \n182: No partial strict mode. All strict flags must be enabled.\n183: \n184: ### Module System Matches Package Boundary\n185: \n186: ```markdown\n187: [ ] TypeScript module settings match the owning workspace/package\n188: ```\n189: \n190: **Current system-spec-kit baseline**:\n191: - `shared/` and `mcp-server/`: package `\"type\": \"module\"` plus\n192:   `module: \"nodenext\"` and `moduleResolution: \"nodenext\"`.\n193: - `scripts/`: package `\"type\": \"module\"` plus `module: \"es2022\"` and\n194:   `moduleResolution: \"node\"`.\n195: - Root `tsconfig.json`: CommonJS fallback for workspaces that do not override it.\n196: \n197: Do not describe CommonJS output as the universal TypeScript baseline.\n198: \n199: ### No Non-Null Assertions Without Justification\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] Non-null assertions (!) have a justification comment on the preceding line\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```typescript\n207: // Map guaranteed to contain key after initialization phase (line 42)\n208: const value = map.get(key)!;\n209: ```\n210: \n211: **Wrong**:\n212: ```typescript\n213: const value = map.get(key)!;  // No explanation\n214: ```\n215: \n216: ### TSDoc on Public API\n217: \n218: ```markdown\n219: [ ] Exported functions, classes, and interfaces have TSDoc comments\n220: ```\n221: \n222: **Minimum**:\n223: ```typescript\n224: /** Search memories matching the given query. */\n225: export async function searchMemories(query: string): Promise<SearchResult[]> { }\n226: ```\n227: \n228: **Full (for complex functions)**:\n229: ```typescript\n230: /**\n231:  * Search memories matching the given query.\n232:  *\n233:  * @param query - Search query text\n234:  * @param options - Search configuration\n235:  * @returns Matching memories sorted by relevance\n236:  * @throws {@link MemoryError} If database unavailable\n237:  */\n238: ```\n239: \n240: ### Typed Error Handling\n241: \n242: ```markdown\n243: [ ] catch blocks use `unknown` type and narrow with instanceof\n244: ```\n245: \n246: **Correct**:\n247: ```typescript\n248: try { } catch (error: unknown) {\n249:   if (error instanceof MemoryError) { }\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: **Wrong**:\n254: ```typescript\n255: try { } catch (error) {        // implicitly any\n256:   console.log(error.message);  // unsafe access\n257: }\n258: ```\n259: \n260: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n261: \n262: ```markdown\n263: [ ] KISS: remove unnecessary abstraction layers\n264: [ ] DRY: duplicate constants/rules are centralized\n265: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP risks reviewed with evidence\n266: ```\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n271: \n272: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n273: \n274: ### Utility Types Used Where Appropriate\n275: \n276: ```markdown\n277: [ ] Standard utility types preferred over manual type construction\n278: ```\n279: \n280: **Correct**:\n281: ```typescript\n282: function update(overrides: Partial<Config>): Config { }\n283: type Summary = Pick<MemoryRecord, 'id' | 'text'>;\n284: ```\n285: \n286: **Less optimal**:\n287: ```typescript\n288: function update(overrides: { timeout?: number; limit?: number }): Config { }\n289: // Manual partial instead of Partial<Config>\n290: ```\n291: \n292: ### Discriminated Unions for Complex State\n293: \n294: ```markdown\n295: [ ] Multi-state objects use discriminated unions instead of optional properties\n296: ```\n297: \n298: **Correct**:\n299: ```typescript\n300: type Result<T> =\n301:   | { success: true; data: T }\n302:   | { success: false; error: string };\n303: ```\n304: \n305: **Less optimal**:\n306: ```typescript\n307: interface Result<T> {\n308:   success: boolean;\n309:   data?: T;         // unclear when present\n310:   error?: string;   // unclear when present\n311: }\n312: ```\n313: \n314: ### Type-Only Imports Separated\n315: \n316: ```markdown\n317: [ ] Imports used only in type positions use `import type`\n318: ```\n319: \n320: **Correct**:\n321: ```typescript\n322: import { MemoryError } from './errors';          // runtime value\n323: import type { SearchResult } from '../types';     // type only\n324: ```\n325: \n326: **Less optimal**:\n327: ```typescript\n328: import { MemoryError, SearchResult } from './module';\n329: // SearchResult only used as type but imported as value\n330: ```\n331: \n332: ### Generic Constraints Where Applicable\n333: \n334: ```markdown\n335: [ ] Generic type parameters have constraints when meaningful\n336: ```\n337: \n338: **Correct**:\n339: ```typescript\n340: function findById<T extends { id: string }>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined { }\n341: ```\n342: \n343: **Less optimal**:\n344: ```typescript\n345: function findById<T>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined { }\n346: // No guarantee T has an 'id' property\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Readonly Where Appropriate\n350: \n351: ```markdown\n352: [ ] Configuration objects and immutable data use readonly modifiers\n353: ```\n354: \n355: **Correct**:\n356: ```typescript\n357: interface SearchConfig {\n358:   readonly maxResults: number;\n359:   readonly timeout: number;\n360: }\n361: ```\n362: \n363: ---\n364: \n365: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n366: \n367: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as TypeScript surface evidence.\n368: \n369: ```markdown\n370: ## TypeScript Standards Surface Evidence\n371: \n372: - [ ] TypeScript standards validated in `typescript-checklist.md`\n373: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n374: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n375: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n376: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n377: ```\n378: \n379: ---\n380: \n381: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n382: \n383: ```bash\n384: # Type check without emitting files\n385: tsc --noEmit\n386: \n387: # Build with project references\n388: tsc --build\n389: \n390: # Check for `any` in public API\n391: grep -rn ': any' src/ --include='*.ts' | grep -v 'node_modules'\n392: \n393: # Check for missing return types on exports\n394: grep -rn 'export function' src/ --include='*.ts' | grep -v '): '\n395: \n396: # Check for untyped catch blocks\n397: grep -rn 'catch (error)' src/ --include='*.ts'\n398: ```\n399: \n400: ---\n401: \n402: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n403: \n404: ### Checklists\n405: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n406: \n407: ### Style Guide\n408: - [TypeScript Style Guide](../../references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md)\n409: - [TypeScript Quality Standards](../../references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md, lines 1-409\n1: ---\n2: title: TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for TypeScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode typescript checklist\"\n6:   - \"typescript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"no any public api\"\n8:   - \"pascalcase types check\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # TypeScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a TypeScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for TypeScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to TypeScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning       | Enforcement                        |\n33: |-------|---------------|------------------------------------|\n34: | P0    | HARD BLOCKER  | Must fix before commit             |\n35: | P1    | Required      | Must fix or get explicit approval  |\n36: | P2    | Recommended   | Can defer with justification       |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### File Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has TypeScript module header identifying the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: A `.d.ts`, `.d.mts` or `.d.cts` file the compiler emitted, one that ends with a `//# sourceMappingURL=` line, is exempt. A hand-written declaration file still needs the header.\n51: \n52: **Required format**:\n53: ```typescript\n54: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n55: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n56: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n57: ```\n58: \n59: ### No `any` in Public API\n60: \n61: ```markdown\n62: [ ] No `any` type used in exported functions, interfaces, or type aliases\n63: ```\n64: \n65: **Correct**:\n66: ```typescript\n67: export function parse(input: unknown): SearchConfig { }\n68: export interface SearchOptions { metadata: Record<string, unknown>; }\n69: ```\n70: \n71: **Wrong**:\n72: ```typescript\n73: export function parse(input: any): any { }\n74: export interface SearchOptions { metadata: any; }\n75: ```\n76: \n77: **Exception**: Interop with untyped third-party modules requires justification comment:\n78: ```typescript\n79: // Interop: sqlite-vec has no type definitions\n80: // eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any\n81: const sqliteVec = require('sqlite-vec') as any;\n82: ```\n83: \n84: ### No Commented-Out Code\n85: \n86: ```markdown\n87: [ ] No commented-out code blocks present\n88: ```\n89: \n90: Same rule as JavaScript. Delete unused code; git preserves history.\n91: \n92: ### WHY Comments\n93: \n94: ```markdown\n95: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n96:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n97:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n98: ```\n99: \n100: Explain reasoning, not mechanics.\n101: \n102: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n103: \n104: ```markdown\n105: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n106: ```\n107: \n108: Same rule as JavaScript.\n109: \n110: ---\n111: \n112: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n113: \n114: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n115: \n116: ### PascalCase Types and Interfaces\n117: \n118: ```markdown\n119: [ ] All interfaces, type aliases, and enums use PascalCase naming\n120: ```\n121: \n122: **Correct**:\n123: ```typescript\n124: interface SearchResult { }\n125: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n126: enum ErrorCode { NotFound, Timeout }\n127: ```\n128: \n129: **Wrong**:\n130: ```typescript\n131: interface searchResult { }       // camelCase\n132: type memoryState = string;       // camelCase\n133: enum errorCode { }               // camelCase\n134: ```\n135: \n136: ### Explicit Return Types on Public Functions\n137: \n138: ```markdown\n139: [ ] All exported functions have explicit return type annotations\n140: ```\n141: \n142: **Correct**:\n143: ```typescript\n144: export function calculate(input: number): number { }\n145: export async function search(query: string): Promise<SearchResult[]> { }\n146: ```\n147: \n148: **Wrong**:\n149: ```typescript\n150: export function calculate(input: number) { }      // inferred return\n151: export async function search(query: string) { }    // inferred return\n152: ```\n153: \n154: Private/internal helper functions may use inferred return types.\n155: \n156: ### Interfaces for Data Shapes\n157: \n158: ```markdown\n159: [ ] Object shapes passed across module boundaries use named interfaces\n160: ```\n161: \n162: **Correct**:\n163: ```typescript\n164: interface SearchOptions {\n165:   limit: number;\n166:   offset: number;\n167: }\n168: function search(query: string, options: SearchOptions): void { }\n169: ```\n170: \n171: **Wrong**:\n172: ```typescript\n173: function search(query: string, options: { limit: number; offset: number }): void { }\n174: ```\n175: \n176: ### Strict Mode Enabled\n177: \n178: ```markdown\n179: [ ] tsconfig.json has \"strict\": true\n180: ```\n181: \n182: No partial strict mode. All strict flags must be enabled.\n183: \n184: ### Module System Matches Package Boundary\n185: \n186: ```markdown\n187: [ ] TypeScript module settings match the owning workspace/package\n188: ```\n189: \n190: **Current system-spec-kit baseline**:\n191: - `shared/` and `mcp-server/`: package `\"type\": \"module\"` plus\n192:   `module: \"nodenext\"` and `moduleResolution: \"nodenext\"`.\n193: - `scripts/`: package `\"type\": \"module\"` plus `module: \"es2022\"` and\n194:   `moduleResolution: \"node\"`.\n195: - Root `tsconfig.json`: CommonJS fallback for workspaces that do not override it.\n196: \n197: Do not describe CommonJS output as the universal TypeScript baseline.\n198: \n199: ### No Non-Null Assertions Without Justification\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] Non-null assertions (!) have a justification comment on the preceding line\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```typescript\n207: // Map guaranteed to contain key after initialization phase (line 42)\n208: const value = map.get(key)!;\n209: ```\n210: \n211: **Wrong**:\n212: ```typescript\n213: const value = map.get(key)!;  // No explanation\n214: ```\n215: \n216: ### TSDoc on Public API\n217: \n218: ```markdown\n219: [ ] Exported functions, classes, and interfaces have TSDoc comments\n220: ```\n221: \n222: **Minimum**:\n223: ```typescript\n224: /** Search memories matching the given query. */\n225: export async function searchMemories(query: string): Promise<SearchResult[]> { }\n226: ```\n227: \n228: **Full (for complex functions)**:\n229: ```typescript\n230: /**\n231:  * Search memories matching the given query.\n232:  *\n233:  * @param query - Search query text\n234:  * @param options - Search configuration\n235:  * @returns Matching memories sorted by relevance\n236:  * @throws {@link MemoryError} If database unavailable\n237:  */\n238: ```\n239: \n240: ### Typed Error Handling\n241: \n242: ```markdown\n243: [ ] catch blocks use `unknown` type and narrow with instanceof\n244: ```\n245: \n246: **Correct**:\n247: ```typescript\n248: try { } catch (error: unknown) {\n249:   if (error instanceof MemoryError) { }\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: **Wrong**:\n254: ```typescript\n255: try { } catch (error) {        // implicitly any\n256:   console.log(error.message);  // unsafe access\n257: }\n258: ```\n259: \n260: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n261: \n262: ```markdown\n263: [ ] KISS: remove unnecessary abstraction layers\n264: [ ] DRY: duplicate constants/rules are centralized\n265: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP risks reviewed with evidence\n266: ```\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n271: \n272: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n273: \n274: ### Utility Types Used Where Appropriate\n275: \n276: ```markdown\n277: [ ] Standard utility types preferred over manual type construction\n278: ```\n279: \n280: **Correct**:\n281: ```typescript\n282: function update(overrides: Partial<Config>): Config { }\n283: type Summary = Pick<MemoryRecord, 'id' | 'text'>;\n284: ```\n285: \n286: **Less optimal**:\n287: ```typescript\n288: function update(overrides: { timeout?: number; limit?: number }): Config { }\n289: // Manual partial instead of Partial<Config>\n290: ```\n291: \n292: ### Discriminated Unions for Complex State\n293: \n294: ```markdown\n295: [ ] Multi-state objects use discriminated unions instead of optional properties\n296: ```\n297: \n298: **Correct**:\n299: ```typescript\n300: type Result<T> =\n301:   | { success: true; data: T }\n302:   | { success: false; error: string };\n303: ```\n304: \n305: **Less optimal**:\n306: ```typescript\n307: interface Result<T> {\n308:   success: boolean;\n309:   data?: T;         // unclear when present\n310:   error?: string;   // unclear when present\n311: }\n312: ```\n313: \n314: ### Type-Only Imports Separated\n315: \n316: ```markdown\n317: [ ] Imports used only in type positions use `import type`\n318: ```\n319: \n320: **Correct**:\n321: ```typescript\n322: import { MemoryError } from './errors';          // runtime value\n323: import type { SearchResult } from '../types';     // type only\n324: ```\n325: \n326: **Less optimal**:\n327: ```typescript\n328: import { MemoryError, SearchResult } from './module';\n329: // SearchResult only used as type but imported as value\n330: ```\n331: \n332: ### Generic Constraints Where Applicable\n333: \n334: ```markdown\n335: [ ] Generic type parameters have constraints when meaningful\n336: ```\n337: \n338: **Correct**:\n339: ```typescript\n340: function findById<T extends { id: string }>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined { }\n341: ```\n342: \n343: **Less optimal**:\n344: ```typescript\n345: function findById<T>(items: T[], id: string): T | undefined { }\n346: // No guarantee T has an 'id' property\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Readonly Where Appropriate\n350: \n351: ```markdown\n352: [ ] Configuration objects and immutable data use readonly modifiers\n353: ```\n354: \n355: **Correct**:\n356: ```typescript\n357: interface SearchConfig {\n358:   readonly maxResults: number;\n359:   readonly timeout: number;\n360: }\n361: ```\n362: \n363: ---\n364: \n365: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n366: \n367: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as TypeScript surface evidence.\n368: \n369: ```markdown\n370: ## TypeScript Standards Surface Evidence\n371: \n372: - [ ] TypeScript standards validated in `typescript-checklist.md`\n373: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n374: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n375: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n376: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n377: ```\n378: \n379: ---\n380: \n381: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n382: \n383: ```bash\n384: # Type check without emitting files\n385: tsc --noEmit\n386: \n387: # Build with project references\n388: tsc --build\n389: \n390: # Check for `any` in public API\n391: grep -rn ': any' src/ --include='*.ts' | grep -v 'node_modules'\n392: \n393: # Check for missing return types on exports\n394: grep -rn 'export function' src/ --include='*.ts' | grep -v '): '\n395: \n396: # Check for untyped catch blocks\n397: grep -rn 'catch (error)' src/ --include='*.ts'\n398: ```\n399: \n400: ---\n401: \n402: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n403: \n404: ### Checklists\n405: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n406: \n407: ### Style Guide\n408: - [TypeScript Style Guide](../../references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md)\n409: - [TypeScript Quality Standards](../../references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560446866,"end":1794560447258}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560447261,"sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4235024001oy0I3eU15IXkml","sessionID":"ses_e2bde2a21ffe162q7QQoGTpivB","messageID":"msg_1d423427c001sNSrTmXA3lQTJf","type":"tool","id":"call_681e24fd3d0040c98b394145","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist